## Founding & History
Bundaleera Vineyard was established in 1996. It was founded by David and Jan Jenkinson, who planted 2.5 hectares of vines on their 12-hectare property. The vineyard is situated on a sunny, sheltered north to northeast slope in the North Esk Valley. More recently, around 2016 (four years prior to a May 2020 interview), Rob Bennett and his wife purchased the property.

## People
Bundaleera Vineyard is currently owned and operated by Rob Bennett and his wife. Jan Jenkinson was previously the full-time viticulturist and gardener for the property. The current owners, Rob and his wife, are a family-run operation. Rob Bennett mentioned in a 2020 interview that he was semi-retired from his glazing business, and his wife worked three days a week at the hospital, allowing them to work the vineyard. They receive help from a retired farmer neighbor and their son when he visits. Andrew Pirie of Pirie Consulting is listed as the winemaker. Rob Bennett also mentioned selling Pinot to Joseph Chromy and Chardonnay and Riesling to Sue (presumably Sue Bell of Bellwether Wines, given the context of winemakers mentioned).

## Vineyard & Site
The vineyard is approximately 25 years old as of a 2020 interview. It has just under two hectares of vines planted. Earlier information from 2007 stated 2.5 hectares of vines. The vineyard is nestled in the hills of Relbia, on the edge of the North Esk River in Tasmania, providing a micro-environment for growing premium grapes. The vines are planted on a north to northeast slope. Varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, and Riesling. Pinot Noir vines constitute half of the vineyard, and Chardonnay vines make up just under a quarter, with 15 rows. The property spans 12 hectares, offering protection from the urban sprawl of Launceston.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door is open most Sundays for tastings and sales, with no bookings required for these times. Private tastings on other days can be arranged by appointment. The cellar door offers a relaxed wine tasting experience. Visitors can build their own picnic from a selection of local cheeses and deli meats available in the Bundaleera larder, or they are welcome to bring their own picnic (excluding outside drinks). The cellar door and vineyard grounds are available for hire for smaller gatherings such as weddings, birthdays, bridal and baby showers, and Christmas parties.

## Food & Hospitality
Bundaleera Vineyard offers farm-stay accommodation. Visitors can build their own picnic from a selection of local cheese and deli meats in the larder. Grazing boxes are also available, requiring at least three days' advance order. Guests are permitted to bring their own picnics, but not their own drinks. The vineyard grounds can be hired for small gatherings.

## Awards & Recognition
Bundaleera Vineyard's Sparkling Non Vintage received 91 points (silver) from James Halliday's Australian Wine Companion. Their Southern Flinders Ranges Sparkling Shiraz Non Vintage Sparkling received 87 points (bronze).

## Sustainability & Certifications
No reliable information found from public sources.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Bundaleera Vineyard is 65 Correa Drive, Relbia, Tasmania, 7258, Australia. It is located just outside Launceston, approximately a 15-minute drive from the city. The phone number listed is 0417 560 592. An older listing shows 0407 366 692. The website is www.bundaleerawines.com.au. An email address is bundaleera@outlook.com. Bundaleera Vineyard is situated at the southern end of the Tamar Valley Wine Route.

## Notable / Distinctive
Bundaleera, meaning 'among the trees', is a small, family-run vineyard that also offers farm-stay accommodation. Visitors have the opportunity to engage directly with the owners and operators to discuss the wines and daily vineyard operations in an authentic and relaxed atmosphere. The vineyard is nestled on the edge of the North Esk River, providing a distinct micro-environment for grape growing. The owners, Rob and his wife, purchased the vineyard in 2016, initially considering bulldozing the vines to just enjoy the view before deciding to embrace viticulture.

